  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(335.35pt, 115.34pt, " In sharp contrast, a clear hysteresis ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 130.94pt, "appears in the range of around ± 20 mT when ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(327.79pt, 130.94pt, "V")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(335.11pt, 131.9pt, "gate")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(348.55pt, 130.94pt, " is tuned to near the broadened ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 146.54pt, "conductance peak (from 3 V to 15 V). Moreover, the resistance of the device always ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 162.14pt, "remains high at small magnetic fields, and switches suddenly to a low value once the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 177.74pt, "fields exceed above 20 mT. Both observations coincide with the typical features of ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 193.34pt, "magnetic order reversal, and motivate us to take a closer look of these magneto ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 208.94pt, "responses.  ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 240.14pt, "During the field sweeping at")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(234.53pt, 240.14pt, " V")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(246.77pt, 241.1pt, "gate")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(260.33pt, 240.14pt, " = 3 V, the resistance is gradually increased and ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 255.77pt, "followed by a steep drop to a stable value (see figure 3c). This response resembles the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 271.37pt, "typical magnetization reversal behavior of a ferromagnet, which could be qualitatively ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 286.97pt, "attributed to the effect of anisotropic magnetoresistance (AMR). As predicted ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 302.57pt, "previously, intrinsic net moments could originate from ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(358.75pt, 302.57pt, "s")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(363.43pt, 302.57pt, "–")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(369.43pt, 302.57pt, "p")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(375.43pt, 302.57pt, " electrons at the two edges ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 318.17pt, "of zGNR. In a macro-spin view, it can be understood that within one edge, these net ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 333.77pt, "moments are paralleled aligned to form a uniform magnetization with magneto-")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 349.37pt, "crystalline anisotropy along the out-of-plane direction. While the magnetizations ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 364.97pt, "between the two edges are antiferromagnetically coupled that can only be rotated at a ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 380.57pt, "magnetic field exceeding the coupling strength. To further validate the explanation, we ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 396.17pt, "performed additional field sweeping transport measurements at 4 K while changing the ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 411.77pt, "direction to either ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(176.45pt, 411.77pt, "x")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(181.73pt, 411.77pt, "- or ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "italic", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(200.57pt, 411.77pt, "y")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(205.85pt, 411.77pt, "-axis (see ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.25pt, 411.77pt, "Fig. S3")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(288.05pt, 411.77pt, "). The disappearance of discernable hysteresis ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 427.39pt, "in both responses supports the existence of magnetism as well as an out-of-plane ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 442.99pt, "anisotropy in the zGNR’s edge state. In this case, the two edge state magnetizations are ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 458.59pt, "rotated in the same way by the moderate external field, so that their antiparallel ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 474.19pt, "configuration is always maintained during the whole sweep. Therefore, zGNR could be ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 489.79pt, "treated equivalently as one ferromagnet, which naturally results in the AMR-like curve. ")
  #line-at(90.024pt, 505.39pt, "Similar MR responses are also found when")
